==English==
{{was wotd|2024|October|17}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵenh₁-}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|genetif|t=pertaining to the genitive case; pertaining to the generation of offspring}},<ref name="MED">{{R:MED Online|entry=ǧenetī̆f|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=MED18386}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|genetif}}, {{m|xno|genitif}}, and {{der|en|frm|genetif}}, {{m|frm|genitif|t=pertaining to the generation of offspring, procreative; (''grammar'') pertaining to the genitive case}} (modern {{cog|fr|génitif}}), and from their {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|genetīvus|t=pertaining to the generation of offspring; (''grammar'') pertaining to the genitive case}} (whence {{cog|LL.|genitivus}}), from {{m|la|genitus|t=begotten, engendered; produced}} + {{m|la|-īvus|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming adjectives with the sense ‘doing’ or ‘related to doing’}}.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=7780644880|date=September 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj.'' and ''n}}</ref> {{m|la||Genitus}} is the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|gignō|t=to beget, give birth to; to produce, yield}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ǵenh₁-|t=to beget, give birth; to produce}}.

{{cog|la|[[genetīvus]] [[casus]]}} (or {{m|la|[[cāsus]] [[genetīvus]]}}, {{m|la|[[cāsus]] [[genitīvus]]|lit=grammatical case pertaining to birth or origin}}), was used to translate {{cog|grc-koi|[[γενῐκή]] [[πτῶσις]]|lit=inflection expressing a kind}} which actually means “generic case”, though it refers to the genitive case.<ref name="OED"/>

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|genetif|t=genitive case}},<ref name="MED"/> from the adjective (see above). Compare {{cog|frm|genitif}} (modern {{cog|fr|génitif}}) and {{cog|la|genetīvus}} (short for {{m|la|genetīvus casus|t=genitive case}}).<ref name="OED"/>

